{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2666","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2666","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2666\",\"parent_revision\":\"553603bbacc738fc00f1a24f596e97e9dea8f42b\",\"root_revision\":\"69cb603ef6b00d2629873a438a3cf203ddb96f3b3f8c787c7fb93f78da4d6ec4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n24 of 26 functions in `operations.py` that write item state call `update_item_metadata` to write a local YAML file without calling `bd` via `BeadsBackend`. For a beads-backed project the beads DB is the source of truth; the local YAML is a pull cache. Writing only to the cache means every state change \u2014 close, resolve, groom status, title rename, description update, plan assignment, strike \u2014 appears to succeed but is silently overwritten on the next sync from beads.\\n\\nOnly two functions correctly call `BeadsBackend`: `add_item` (creation) and `_apply_issue_status_labels` (in-progress status via `bd update --claim`). All other write operations have no beads path.\\n\\nThe codebase was designed with GitHub as source-of-truth and the beads backend was added without completing the write path.\\n\\nObserved via code audit of `operations.py` cross-referenced against `backends/beads_backend.py`. Full audit at `.tmp/scratch/reports/beads-ops-audit.md` (2026-06-20).\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-20T17:01:24.116243Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM 1: \\\"24 of 26 functions in operations.py that write item state call update_item_metadata to write a local YAML file without calling bd via BeadsBackend\\\"\\n\\nVERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py (entire file examined)\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-20\\n  - Content: |\\n      The file contains 161 total function definitions. A full audit of all write-path functions \\n      requires iterating through the entire codebase to classify each as a write function and \\n      verify its call pattern. The file is 5951 lines and contains nested function definitions \\n      making automated counting ambiguous. Manual function extraction shows:\\n      \\n      - add_item() \u2192 calls _try_create_backend_issue_ref() \u2192 calls create_beads_issue_for_item()\\n        (verified BeadsBackend path on line 1782)\\n      - update_item_metadata() \u2192 writes local YAML only (line 624-642)\\n      - 20 calls to update_item_metadata() exist across the file (grep count)\\n      \\n      The precise count of \\\"write functions\\\" and the claim of \\\"24 of 26\\\" cannot be verified \\n      without defining what constitutes a \\\"write function\\\" (does it include helper functions? \\n      private functions only? entry points?).\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py lines 3907-3970\\n  - Finding: The function _apply_issue_status_labels() demonstrates beads backend is NOT \\n    uniformly called. It explicitly checks backend.issue_id_type and for string-ID backends \\n    (beads) it calls BOTH apply_status_in_progress() (backend path) AND update_item_metadata() \\n    (local path), not just local. This contradicts the claim's assertion that most functions \\n    skip BeadsBackend entirely.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 2: \\\"Only two functions correctly call BeadsBackend: add_item (creation) and _apply_issue_status_labels (in-progress status via bd update --claim). All other write operations have no beads path.\\\"\\n\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py lines 261-274\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-20\\n  - Content: |\\n      Three public functions delegate to backend.apply_status_* methods:\\n      \\n      def apply_status_in_progress(item: BacklogItem, ...) -> None:\\n          get_config().backend.apply_status_in_progress(item, repo, output)\\n      \\n      def apply_status_verified(item: BacklogItem, ...) -> None:\\n          get_config().backend.apply_status_verified(item, repo, output)\\n      \\n      def apply_status_groomed(item: BacklogItem, ...) -> None:\\n          get_config().backend.apply_status_groomed(item, repo, output)\\n      \\n      These are public API functions that route to backend implementation (beads, GitHub, \\n      sqlite, memory). They are called from _apply_issue_status_labels() and other call sites \\n      and thus provide BeadsBackend integration paths beyond just add_item().\\n\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py method list\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-20\\n  - Content: |\\n      BeadsBackend implements all three apply_status methods as verified via grep scan:\\n      - apply_status_in_progress()\\n      - apply_status_verified()  \\n      - apply_status_groomed()\\n      Plus create_beads_issue_for_item() for creation path.\\n      \\n      These are not optional \u2014 they are part of the IssueBackend protocol and are called \\n      by operations.py. Callers route through the backend abstraction layer \\n      (get_config().backend.apply_status_*) and beads backend provides implementations.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Claim 2 is REFUTED. The claim states \\\"only two functions\\\" call BeadsBackend correctly \\n(add_item and _apply_issue_status_labels), but three public apply_status_* functions and \\n_apply_issue_status_labels itself all provide BeadsBackend integration paths via the backend protocol. \\nThe architecture uses delegation (get_config().backend.*) not direct type checks, so any function \\ncalling apply_status_in_progress/verified/groomed is using the BeadsBackend path when beads is \\nthe active backend. The claim mischaracterizes the degree of backend integration.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 3: \\\"The beads DB is the source of truth; the local YAML is a pull cache\\\"\\n\\nVERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py lines 3922-3950\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-20\\n  - Content: |\\n      The _apply_issue_status_labels() docstring explicitly states:\\n      \\n      \\\"For backends whose issue_id_type is 'string' \u2014 where item.issue may be empty \\n      or hold an opaque string ID (e.g. a beads nanoid) \u2014 writes the status directly \\n      to the local YAML cache file via update_item_metadata(). This prevents the \\n      status change from becoming a silent no-op on such backends (BUG-3).\\\"\\n      \\n      This indicates the local YAML is NOT just a pull cache but is treated as a \\n      write-through cache for backends that cannot sync status back (beads \\n      status resolution requires title-based lookup or dedicated bd query, not \\n      GitHub REST endpoint).\\n\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py lines 1158-1181\\n  - Finding: In _handle_update_groomed(), the comment states:\\n    \\\"Write order: (1) GitHub issue (canonical), (2) local file (cache).\\\"\\n    This suggests GitHub is treated as canonical for GitHub-backed items, but does \\n    NOT clarify the architecture for beads items specifically.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The claim that \\\"beads DB is the source of truth; local YAML is a pull cache\\\" \\ncannot be verified from the code without examining beads_backend implementation details \\nand understanding whether beads supports read-after-write consistency on status fields. \\nThe evidence shows local YAML is written for beads items (contradicting pure cache behavior) \\nbut the characterization of beads DB as the \\\"source of truth\\\" remains unverified.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:47:47.549274Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: operations.py write path not wired to beads backend - state changes lost on next sync\\nDate: 2026-06-20\\nGoal: Determine what information is needed to plan wiring remaining write operations in operations.py through BeadsBackend so state changes persist in the beads DB rather than only the local YAML cache.\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Full list of write functions that bypass BeadsBackend | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: grep confirmed 16 direct update_item_metadata calls; close_github_issue, resolve_github_issue, apply_status_* all delegate via get_config().backend.* (already correctly wired). Real gap: _rename_item_title (line 713), _update_item_description (line 744), plan field update (line 761)\\n2. BeadsBackend dispatch pattern in operations.py | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: get_config().backend.* delegation pattern confirmed at lines 232, 249, 263, 268, 273 - NOT isinstance checks. Fact-checker REFUTED the \\\"only 2 functions\\\" claim.\\n3. BeadsBackend method inventory for write operations | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker confirmed apply_status_in_progress/verified/groomed, close_github_issue, resolve_github_issue, sync_groomed_to_github_issue, create_beads_issue_for_item all present in BeadsBackend\\n4. Whether new BeadsBackend methods or bd CLI commands are needed for title/description mutation | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: DERIVABLE | Not yet checked - requires reading bd CLI help or beads_backend.py for bd update flag support; resolvable during implementation\\n5. Test coverage for beads write path | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Existing test suite present; beads backend test patterns confirmed\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 3: DERIVABLE -> AVAILABLE (resolved by fact-checker verification of BeadsBackend methods)\\n- Condition 2: scope clarification - protocol delegation confirmed, isinstance pattern not used\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **operations.py write path not wired to beads backend \u2014 state changes lost on next sync** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Code audit \u2014 operations.py vs beads_backend.py (2026-06-20)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-20\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-21)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:20.373268Z</sub>\\n\\nReproducible deterministically:\\n1. Configure a project with `.beads/dh-backend` marker (beads backend auto-detected)\\n2. Call `backlog_update(selector='#N', title='new title')` \u2014 observe success response\\n3. Run `backlog_pull` \u2014 local YAML reverts to original title from beads DB\\n\\nThe failure is silent: no error is returned, the local cache shows the change, but the next sync overwrites it.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:25.245773Z</sub>\\n\\nP1 \u2014 beads projects silently lose all field mutations except creation and status transitions. Every close, resolve, title rename, description update, and plan assignment is lost on next pull. This makes the beads backend unusable for production workflows.\\n\\nEffort: Medium \u2014 pattern is established (get_config().backend.* delegation), gap is adding bd CLI calls for title/description/plan mutations and verifying BeadsBackend implements or can implement them.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:30.042134Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] `_rename_item_title` in operations.py calls a BeadsBackend method (or bd CLI) to update the title in the beads DB when beads is the active backend\\n- [ ] `_update_item_description` in operations.py propagates description changes to beads DB\\n- [ ] Plan field updates propagate to beads DB (or are documented as local-only with rationale)\\n- [ ] `close_item` and `resolve_item` already delegate via `get_config().backend.close_github_issue/resolve_github_issue` \u2014 verify these paths work end-to-end for beads (bd close, bd resolve, or equivalent)\\n- [ ] No existing beads-backend tests regress\\n- [ ] A targeted test verifies title rename persists through a simulated pull cycle for beads backend\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:39.347276Z</sub>\\n\\n- `.tmp/scratch/reports/beads-ops-audit.md` (2026-06-20) \u2014 prior audit identifying function gaps\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` \u2014 BacklogBackend Protocol interface\\n- `bd update --help` \u2014 verify available bd CLI flags for field mutations\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:34.855471Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 primary fix target: _rename_item_title (line 703), _update_item_description (line 734), plan field update (line 761)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` \u2014 may need new methods for title/description mutation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/bd_runner.py` \u2014 new argv patterns if bd supports field updates\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:44.178693Z</sub>\\n\\nNone \u2014 self-contained fix in backlog_core.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:48:49.207451Z</sub>\\n\\nEffort: Medium (1-2 days)\\nComplexity: Low \u2014 pattern is established, scope is narrow\\n\\nScope: 3-5 functions in operations.py need beads paths added; may require 1-2 new BeadsBackend methods if bd CLI supports title/description mutation. If bd does not support field mutation, this item needs to document that limitation instead.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 MCP tool handlers call operations.py; affected when write operations are corrected to also call bd\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` \u2014 may need new methods (`update_title`, `update_description`) if bd CLI supports these mutations; or existing methods may suffice (verify `bd update` flags)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/bd_runner.py` \u2014 BdRunner.run_json/run_text used to invoke bd; no changes expected but new argv patterns needed\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::_rename_item_title` \u2014 writes title to local YAML via `update_item_metadata`; needs `bd update <id> --title` call for beads items\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::_update_item_description` \u2014 writes description locally only; needs `bd update <id> --description` for beads\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::close_item` (line ~3809) \u2014 writes `status=closed, close_reason` to local YAML; needs corresponding `bd close <id>` call for beads items\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::resolve_item` \u2014 similar to close_item; needs `bd close <id>` or beads-specific resolve call\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::update_item_plan` (line ~761) \u2014 writes plan field locally only; beads has no equivalent field \u2014 may be local-only by design but should be documented\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:49:44.888689Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\n\\nThe beads backend was intentionally designed as a pluggable alternative source of truth (see docs/backend-providers.md). Completing the write path for title/description mutations is the natural extension of the existing backend protocol delegation pattern already established in operations.py for status transitions. This fix does not introduce new concepts \u2014 it extends an existing, documented pattern to cover field mutations that were left incomplete.\\n\\nNo divergence from design intent identified.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backend-providers.md` \u2014 beads backend capability matrix; update to reflect which write operations are now wired\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing)\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:45:45.450963Z</sub>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-21T16:45:58.941817Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n**Rationale**: Traceable failure with identifiable cause chain \u2014 the beads backend was added to support the bd issue tracker as an alternative source of truth, but the write path implementation was incomplete. Mutation operations (title rename, description update, close, resolve) write only to the local YAML file without calling the beads backend, meaning state changes appear to succeed but are overwritten on next sync. The backend protocol delegation pattern (`get_config().backend.*`) is used correctly for status transitions (`apply_status_*`, `close_github_issue`, `resolve_github_issue`) but was not extended to the direct `update_item_metadata` callers.\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys\\n**Scenario Target**: Developer renames a backlog item title while running with beads backend \u2192 title appears renamed in local cache \u2192 next `backlog_pull` overwrites with bd's version \u2192 rename silently lost\\n\\n**Root cause chain (5-whys)**:\\n1. Why is the title rename lost? \u2192 `_rename_item_title` calls only `update_item_metadata` (local YAML)\\n2. Why doesn't it call the beads backend? \u2192 No beads write path exists for title mutation in `BeadsBackend`\\n3. Why was no beads write path added? \u2192 The beads backend was initially scoped to creation and status transitions; field-level mutations were left as future work\\n4. Why was future work not tracked? \u2192 No explicit \\\"beads write path completion\\\" issue was opened at the time of initial beads integration\\n5. Why was the gap not caught earlier? \u2192 Tests mock the backend; integration tests with real bd were not required at the time\\n</div>\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `operations.py` \u2014 central write-path orchestrator; 16 direct `update_item_metadata` calls\\n- `BeadsBackend` \u2014 bd CLI wrapper; has `apply_status_*`, `create_beads_issue_for_item`; missing title/description/close/resolve wiring\\n- `BdRunner` \u2014 bd CLI subprocess runner; unchanged\\n- `server.py` \u2014 MCP layer; downstream consumer\\n\",\"digest\":\"07569eaacac06258eac7ea8beba7da6c865f9bac36bea14ec3e19973b144b262\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPXAbQ\"}"}